. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * 
. * This section tests whether having daughters leads to greater gender equalit
> y within the household (H3). 
.                 * 
.                 * Dependent Variable:  
.                 * Gender equality in the household is measured using a contin
> uous outcome variable that captures 
.                 * intra-household economic inequality. Specifically, we calcu
> late the income gap as the difference 
.                 * between the respondent's net individual labor income (pglab
> net) and their partner's net labor 
.                 * income (partner_income, retrieved via merging on partner ID
> ). Singles (parid == -2) are excluded 
.                 * from the analysis as no within-household gap can be compute
> d. Since this variable may include 
.                 * zero and negative values and is skewed, we apply the invers
> e hyperbolic sine (IHS) transformation 
.                 * to normalize its distribution:  
.                 * 
.                 *     gen ihs_income_gap = asinh(pglabnet - partner_income) i
> f !missing(pglabnet, partner_income)
.                 * 
.                 * The IHS transformation behaves similarly to a logarithmic t
> ransformation for large values 
.                 * but remains defined at zero and for negative inputs (Rehm e
> t al., 2022). This approach allows 
.                 * us to retain the full range of observations without imposin
> g arbitrary truncation.  
.                 * 
.                 * Model Specification:  
.                 * We estimate a linear panel model using ordinary least squar
> es (OLS) to test whether having 
.                 * daughters influences the transformed income gap. To control
>  for general parenthood effects, 
.                 * we include fixed effects for the total number of children (
> absorb(tot_kids)). Standard errors 
.                 * are clustered at the household level (hid) to account for r
> epeated observations within households.  
.                 * The model is specified as follows:   
. 
. areg ihs_income_gap daughters_nr `controls', absorb(tot_kids) cluster(hid)

